{
  "title": "CHAPTER 21: PUBLIC FINANCE",
  "order": 20,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "uxbu2tg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO PUBLIC FINANCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Public Finance covers government income, expenditure, deficit management, and public debt to ensure welfare and economic growth.",
        "Direct tax burden cannot be shifted and is progressive, while indirect tax burden is shifted to consumers and is regressive.",
        "GST was introduced in 2017 under 'One Nation One Tax' to eliminate multiple indirect taxes.",
        "Fiscal Deficit indicates total government borrowing requirement, whereas Primary Deficit excludes interest payments on past loans.",
        "The Finance Commission is a constitutional body established under Article 280 to distribute tax revenue between the Centre and States."
      ],
      "id": "of3i2tk"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Components of Public Finance",
          "value": "4 Main Parts",
          "icon": "info"
        },
